  "holdout_metric": null,
  "surprise": "Systems speed idea falsified on its own measurement: packing the same 128 records per step into document-masked rows worked mechanically (packed_rows_mean 63.3/63.4/63.3, about half the 128 padded rows, as predicted) but the step rate FELL, 2430/2484/2230 pretrain steps vs 136-fable's 3370/3351/3367 (-28/-26/-34%; steady ~8.6 vs ~11.8 steps/s from the first curve point on, so not warm-up or allocator growth), against a registered 4400-5800. Metric 574/562/600 (mean 578.67) vs 658 (655/673/646): -79.3, paired -81/-111/-46, far below the registered 0 to +20. The loss math is identical to the padded layout (CPU check: loss equal to 1e-6, grad diff 7.7e-7), so this run is a clean fewer-steps ablation of the incumbent: ~29% fewer steps cost ~79 theorems. Post-pretrain held-out greedy 0.962/0.937/0.958 vs 0.965/0.9705/0.968; final val loss 0.0886/0.0860/0.0780 vs 0.0860/0.0833/0.0788; RL-solved targets after round 4 1043/1024/1085. Why slower is unprofiled. Two points (interpretation, not measured here): 103/124 moved MLP FLOPs by +-17% for only -+4-5% steps, so most of the step is per-block overhead (kernel launches, the unbatched Muon over 30 matrices) rather than tokens, and halving token slots saves little. And packing replaced the batch-broadcast (1,H,T,T) ALiBi bias, which torch 2.4 pads before expanding and which stays L2-resident, with a dense per-row (R,H,L,L) bias: a 170 MB float32 masked_fill plus an 85 MB bf16 copy per block, read from DRAM in every attention forward and backward. That per-row bias is the leading suspect for the ~33 ms/step added. Next speed work should cut launches, not tokens: batch the Muon Newton-Schulz by shape, or use CUDA graphs/compile over a few padded length buckets. Keep the broadcast bias.",
